-- title:
--   Closedness of the locus where im f ⊆ 𝔭 Q
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $P$ and $Q$ be $R$-modules (additive commutative groups equipped with $R$-module structures), with $Q$ assumed both finitely generated and projective over $R$. Let $f : P \to Q$ be an $R$-linear map. The assertion is that the set of points $x$ of $\operatorname{Spec} R$ for which the image submodule $\operatorname{range} f \subseteq Q$ is contained in $\mathfrak p_x \cdot Q$, where $\mathfrak p_x$ is the prime ideal corresponding to $x$ and $\mathfrak p_x \cdot Q$ denotes the product of the ideal $\mathfrak p_x$ with the top submodule of $Q$, is a closed subset of the prime spectrum of $R$ in its Zariski topology. Equivalently, the locus of primes at which $f$ becomes zero after reduction modulo $\mathfrak p_x$ on the target is cut out by equations; no hypothesis is placed on $P$, and $f$ is an arbitrary linear map.
--
--   This is the standard fact that, for a finite projective target, the condition $\operatorname{im} f \subseteq \mathfrak p Q$ is detected by the vanishing of the ideal generated by the values of linear forms on $\operatorname{im} f$, so that the locus in question is a Zariski-closed set. It is used to exhibit the strata of a chart-local Drinfeld quadruple in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, where two statements about quadruples attached to a Deligne datum on the formal upper half plane invoke it.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Module_isClosed_setOf_range_le_smul_top.lean

